      "writtenOn": "2025-04-02T00:58:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This doesn\u0027t immediately make sense to me. If it can\u0027t be updated while read, there is no reason to make it volatile. If it can be, it should be atomic. If you want to avoid the compiler combining atomic loads, it should arguably be both volatile and atomic, though it would be a bit strange to count on that for actual correctness. Normally things are just volatile if the value can change due to factors outside the standardized language (IO, changes through a separate memory mapping) or if you want the value to be stored naively for debugging purposes. Thread interactions generally require \"atomic\". That\u0027s really also the cleanest way to handle signal handler interactions, those those waters are muddied a bit by sig_atomic_t.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 827,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 829,
